    Hello.

    Petr's routines must have worked very well, but didn't know about RGB
monitors, and as I didn't have a VGA monitor until very recently, they 
weren't much help to me.

    So I decided to rewrite the Videl routines so they'd work with my
Falcon. I didn't bank on rewriting them completely... but I have. Big
thanks to evl/dhs, who released heavily tested Videl routines with
contributions from chris/aura, scandion/mugwumps, and sage/escape, on
which I based mine. (The original routines wouldn't work if ST-compatible
resolutions were involved - the Videl is an arcane chip at best...)

    I also enhanced the detection within screen.c - now, colour terminals
are possible with extended resolutions. (And, I patched paint.c - now all
five text effects are supported, even with the generic paint routines.)

    So, what we have here is a version of vconsd (and derivatives) which
allows all Falcons to have large colour virtual terminals. (Except those
connected to a monochrome - Atari SM124 or 125-type - monitor, which
supports neither extended resolutions or colour <g>.)

    Caveats:

 <> The format of the /etc/resolution file has changed. You'll need to
    make a new one if you used 0.9a, with the new save_res.tos program.
    (Please don't mix the versions! <g>)

 <> ST-low res is falsely detected as having 4 colours. (Surely no-one
    would use a 40-column text terminal, though? <g>)

 <> The default resolutions (when no /etc/resolution is found) for a 
    Falcon are all monochrome. Yes, there is (VT52, as usual; anyone
    willing to add VT100 codes as well?) colour support, but you'll 
    need to save a colour resolution first.

 <> It's tested with my Falcon, with an SC1435 RGB monitor (which is now
    tethered to my PlayStation <g>), a couple of televisions, an SM124
    mono monitor, and a 14 inch SVGA monitor of uncertain pedigree.

 <> Use this at your own risk! Due to the nature of the code, it could
    cause damage to an attached monitor if unsuitable signals are sent
    to it. I haven't experienced damage myself, but I have made my own
    monitor whine a couple of times while developing this, and still
    tend to keep one finger on the reset button when testing a new
    version... you've been warned, it's beta, okay? <g>

 <> Default colours are white on black; I prefer that. Yes, you can
    change it back if you like by editing screen.c to change the palette
    and recompiling.

 <> vconsd can now use 2-, 4-, and 16-colour resolutions. 256-colour
    planar resolutions will work after a fashion, but are strangely 
    pointless as only 16 colours can be used. <g> High-colour (16-bit)
    resolutions *won't* work. Don't even try.

    Basically, keep a backup of your old version, and feel free to
try - and patch! (any daring Hades or Milan owners want to try their
hand at hardware support for the graphic cards?) - this little kludge-
bucket. ;)

Future ideas: 

 <> Possibly allow connection to different monitors with different 
    filenames for each montior? /etc/resolution.rgb, /etc/resolution.vga,
    /etc/resolution.mono, /etc/resolution.tv (delete where appropriate)
    to be checked and used if found, otherwise try /etc/resolution?
    (As I may well swap from VGA to RGB and back again fairly often,
    I might add this myself <grin>)

 -- Steven Moore.  -  smooreg@essex.ac.uk  -  http://sentroid.tsx.org/


... The original README.Falcon file for virtual terminals 0.9a follows...

    Hi Falcon users,

I didn't understand all the time why the great virtual consoles
were limited to dumb 30 lines with only 80 chars.

My Falcon runs in 896x640 with 40 character lines per screen, each 112
characters long. Furthermore the VGA refresh rate is 71 Hz in my case,
which is much more pleasant for eyes than standard 60 Hz.
I use BlowUP's screen enhancer with 50 MHz oscillator as the external
VIDEL clock.

If you would like to run vconsoles in your own resolution, simply 
go to Desktop in 2 color mode. Setup your favourite resolution using
any Falcon screen enhancer (FalconScreen, ScreenBlaster, BlowUP030,
VidelInside or Videlity) and then start SAVE_RES.TOS program.
It will save your resolution into file '/etc/resolution'. The vconsd
from this distribution will then read the file and use the data
for setting the same resolution in vconsoles. If writting to that file
fails (for example because you're in SingleTOS at that moment), the
program writes the data to alternate file in current dir named 'etc_res'.
You must then copy this file manually to the '/etc/resolution' file.

Don't forget to switch into 2-colors mode before running
the SAVE_RES.TOS, because virtual consoles are patched for mono
mode only.

(Steven Moore's note: This is no longer true; the patch is complete.)

The vconsoles-0.9 also include special software patch for unfamous
VIDEL bug (shifted screen when changing resolution). Look for
VIDEL_resync() code (this workaround was found out by a German programmer
and I got it from MiNT (or Falcon040?) mailing list).

Enjoy,

Petr Stehlik (22.3.1997, 14.6.1997, 10.5.1998, 11.5.1998)

